**Read:** [The safe isolation of plant and equipment — HSE HSG253](../sources/Safe-Isolation-HSE.lingua#r_2MDH4HPKRVQ21W24H0ESCKZ13H), reinstatement; [Cleaning equipment for oxygen service — EIGA 33/18](../sources/Cleaning-for-Oxygen-Service-EIGA.lingua#r_27WYRBCNFB89Z5DXR0PMHWNES0), applicable cleanliness evidence; [Reliability-centered maintenance — NASA, September 2008](../sources/Reliability-Centered-Maintenance-NASA.lingua#r_08ZD3ATSYCJX5586EYSY0M4N3R), acceptance and feedback.

After repair, establish the resulting configuration and material state. Correct dimensions or successful motion do not establish leak tightness, composition, surface cleanliness or sensor calibration. A repaired assembly may require evidence at several levels before the original service claim is valid again.

Retain the cause of intervention, as-found observations, changed parts/materials and as-left evidence. A newly introduced component may alter permeability, adsorption, dead volume or thermal response even when nominal pressure and dimensions match.

**Work:** Trace a hypothetical seal replacement through the earlier surface, delivery and measurement models. Identify which original results remain applicable and which must be re-established. Define a return-to-service claim at the point of use and show the evidence supporting it.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
